# Instruction Decode/Execute Stage — Behavioral Specification

## 1. Overview

The instruction decode/execute stage is the execution heart of the processor pipeline. It receives fetched instruction words, decodes them, reads the register file, generates operand values, issues execution requests to the execution and memory systems, and keeps multi-cycle instructions in the pipeline until they can complete architected execution.

The stage must preserve correct architectural behavior for:

- integer, control-transfer, CSR, load/store, multiply/divide, and configured bit-manipulation instructions;
- compressed instruction expansion effects;
- illegal instruction and privilege violations;
- pipeline hazards and stalls;
- branch, jump, exception, interrupt, and debug redirection;
- configurable pipeline modes and security/timing features.

## 2. Instruction Validity and Execution Gating

An instruction fetched into the stage shall only be considered for execution if it is marked valid and does not have an instruction fetch error.

The stage must not perform architectural side effects for an instruction that:

- has an instruction fetch error;
- is an illegal compressed instruction;
- is otherwise decoded as illegal;
- performs an illegal CSR access;
- is not allowed by the current privilege mode;
- is not permitted by the current execution control state.

The stage must produce an illegal instruction indication for a valid fetched instruction when any of the following conditions is true:

- the instruction encoding is not a legal instruction in the configured ISA;
- the instruction contains an illegal compressed encoding;
- an attempted CSR access is illegal;
- a debug-return instruction is executed outside Debug Mode;
- an M-mode-only return instruction is executed in User Mode;
- a WFI instruction is executed in User Mode while the relevant machine-status timeout-wait control is set.

An instruction with a fetch error must not execute, must not write registers, and must not generate memory or CSR requests.

## 3. Decode and Operand Generation

The stage must decode the instruction and generate all immediate values required by the instruction format. It must support standard RISC-V immediate forms and any additional immediate form required by configured extensions.

The stage must supply the execution unit with:

- the ALU operation to perform;
- the operand A value;
- the operand B value.

Operand A shall be selectable from:

- register file read data or forwarded writeback data;
- the current instruction program counter;
- a zero-extended register-immediate operand;
- the previous load/store address when an incremental access is being generated.

Operand B shall be selectable from:

- register file read data or forwarded writeback data;
- the decoded instruction immediate.

When a misaligned load/store is split into multiple accesses, the stage must be able to generate the second access address using the last access address and an address increment.

When the branch-target-ALU configuration is enabled, the stage must also generate a separate branch/jump target operand pair. This target path must be able to use the current program counter or a register operand combined with the appropriate branch or jump immediate.

For compressed instructions, any program-counter-relative increment or fall-through address calculation must use an increment of 2. For uncompressed instructions, it must use an increment of 4.

## 4. Register File Behavior

The stage shall read the register file only for valid, eligible instructions. No register read shall be requested for:

- invalid instructions;
- instructions with fetch errors;
- illegal instructions.

The stage shall provide read addresses for the two source register operands and receive the corresponding read data.

Register writes shall only occur when an instruction is actually executing and completing. A register write must not occur for an instruction with an illegal CSR access. The value written shall be the execution result or the CSR read data, depending on the instruction.

When a writeback stage is present:

- register data from a previous instruction in the writeback stage shall be forwarded when the destination register matches the current source register and the source register is not the zero register;
- if the producing instruction is an outstanding load, forwarding is not possible in the same cycle, and the stage must instead stall the current instruction until the load data is available.

When no writeback stage is present, no writeback forwarding is used; source operands shall come directly from the register file.

The stage must identify when a read register address matches the writeback destination address so that hazard tracking can be performed. Matches involving the zero register shall not create hazards.

## 5. Stalls and Hazards

The stage must hold an instruction in the decode/execute stage whenever the instruction cannot yet complete. Stall conditions include:

- a load-use hazard where an outstanding load will write a register that the current instruction reads;
- an outstanding memory access whose response has not yet arrived;
- a load/store request that has not yet completed;
- a multi-cycle multiply/divide operation that has not yet produced a valid result;
- a multi-cycle ALU operation;
- a jump or taken branch that requires more than one cycle;
- a writeback stage that is not ready to accept the completing instruction;
- a memory exception or fetch exception that must be resolved before the instruction is allowed to execute.

While an instruction is stalled, the stage must not issue duplicate memory requests, must not complete the instruction, and must not allow it to progress to writeback.

If a pipeline flush is asserted, the current instruction shall not complete, even if it would otherwise be ready.

An illegal instruction must not be stalled for reasons unrelated to memory. If an illegal instruction is stalled, the stall must be due to an outstanding memory access or memory error that must take priority.

## 6. Multi-Cycle Instruction Handling

The stage must support instructions that require multiple cycles to complete.

### 6.1 Loads and Stores

The stage must issue load/store requests only when the instruction is actually executing and the memory system is allowed to accept a new request.

In the two-stage pipeline configuration:

- a load or store must be held for at least one cycle;
- it must remain held until the memory system returns a response.

In the three-stage configuration:

- a new load/store request may only be issued if no previous memory access is still outstanding and unresolved;
- the instruction must remain in the decode/execute stage until its own memory request has been completed;
- the stage must not complete when there is a possibility that a previous outstanding memory access will report an error.

Store data must be provided from the second register read port, including any applicable forwarding.

### 6.2 Multiply and Divide

When a multiply/divide instruction is decoded:

- if a valid execution result is available in the first cycle, the instruction may complete in that cycle;
- otherwise, the stage must stall and must not write the result register until the execution unit reports a valid result;
- memory, CSR, and other architectural side effects must be suppressed while the instruction is held.

### 6.3 Multi-Cycle ALU Operations

Certain configured bit-manipulation instructions require an additional cycle. The stage must hold such instructions, suppress register writeback until the result is valid, and stall younger instructions.

### 6.4 Completion

An instruction shall be considered complete only when:

- it is executing;
- no stall condition is active;
- no pipeline flush is active;
- if a writeback stage is present, writeback is ready to accept it;
- no pending exception or unresolved memory access would prevent retirement.

The stage must provide a completion indication for the current instruction. This indication is used to move the instruction to writeback or retire it.

## 7. Branch and Jump Handling

The stage must evaluate conditional branches, unconditional jumps, and instruction-fence operations.

### 7.1 Conditional Branches

A conditional branch shall be treated as not-taken or taken according to the evaluated branch condition.

Execution behavior shall depend on the configuration:

- If the branch-target-ALU path is enabled and data-independent timing is not active, a taken branch may complete in one cycle because the target can be calculated in parallel with the condition.
- If the branch-target-ALU path is not enabled and the branch is taken, an additional cycle is required to compute the target.
- If the branch is not taken, the branch shall not stall unless data-independent timing requires otherwise.
- If data-independent timing is active, all branches must take the same number of cycles regardless of whether they are taken, and the timing must not depend on the branch condition value.

For each branch that needs to redirect fetch, the stage must send a branch-redirect request exactly once. Repeated requests for the same branch must be suppressed.

When branch prediction is enabled, the stage must also provide the not-taken fall-through address so that a mispredicted branch can be corrected.

### 7.2 Jumps

Unconditional jumps shall redirect fetch to the computed target.

If the branch-target-ALU path is enabled, a jump may complete in one cycle. Otherwise, the jump must be held for an additional cycle while the target is calculated.

A jump-redirect request must be signalled exactly once per jump instruction.

### 7.3 Instruction Fence

An instruction-fence operation must behave like a jump for pipeline flushing purposes. It must also request invalidation of the instruction cache so that subsequent instruction fetches observe the new instruction stream.

### 7.4 Branch Event Indication

The stage must produce performance-event indications for branch and jump instructions. It must distinguish:

- jump instructions;
- branch instructions;
- taken branch instructions.

## 8. CSR Behavior

CSR accesses must be generated only for instructions that are actually executing and are in a state where they can complete.

The stage must support CSR read, write, set, and clear operations as decoded. If a CSR access is illegal, the stage must:

- suppress the CSR operation;
- suppress register writeback for the CSR destination;
- report the instruction as illegal.

CSR read data must be available for register file writeback when the instruction completes.

Certain CSR writes require a pipeline flush so that their effects are observed by subsequent instructions. In particular, a flush is required after writes that can change:

- global interrupt enable state;
- individual interrupt enable state;
- physical memory protection configuration or address registers;
- debug control and scratch registers.

The module must not create the CSR operation in a way that depends on the illegal-status result of the same access; the illegal-status determination is made separately from the enable of the actual CSR operation.

## 9. Exceptions, Privilege, and Debug

### 9.1 Privilege Checks

The stage must enforce the following privilege rules:

- Machine-return instructions are only legal in Machine Mode.
- WFI is illegal in User Mode when the machine-status timeout-wait control is set.
- Debug-return instructions are legal only when the core is in Debug Mode.

### 9.2 Exception Ordering and Precise Exceptions

The stage must preserve precise exception behavior for memory errors:

- It must not allow a younger instruction to complete while a previous memory access is still unresolved and could still report an error.
- If an exception is already pending in the writeback stage, the current instruction must not execute.
- If an instruction fetch error is present, the instruction must not execute; the exception shall take priority.

The faulting instruction must be available as the trap value for illegal instruction exceptions.

### 9.3 Debug Interaction

The stage must recognize instructions and conditions relevant to debug:

- debug-return is illegal outside Debug Mode;
- breakpoint instructions and debug-mode entry are handled by the system control logic;
- debug requests and trigger matches must prevent or redirect instruction execution as required by the debug specification.

## 10. Pipeline Configuration Variants

### 10.1 Two-Stage Pipeline (No Writeback Stage)

When no writeback stage is configured:

- the instruction completes at the end of the decode/execute stage;
- there is no writeback forwarding;
- no load-use hazard is detected from a writeback stage;
- loads and stores must stall for at least the first cycle, and then remain stalled until the memory response arrives.

### 10.2 Three-Stage Pipeline (With Writeback Stage)

When a writeback stage is configured:

- an instruction completes only when the writeback stage is ready to accept it;
- register-file write data from the writeback stage is forwarded when possible;
- load-use hazards are detected and cause stalls;
- a new memory access may be issued only when no previous memory access is outstanding;
- the stage must classify completing instructions as loads, stores, or other so that writeback can handle them correctly.

### 10.3 Data-Independent Timing

When data-independent timing is active, instruction execution timing must not reveal the values of branch conditions or branch decisions.

In this mode:

- conditional branches must take a fixed number of cycles independent of whether they are taken or not;
- the branch condition must not affect the address calculation timing;
- the branch outcome used in the following cycle must be the sampled result from the evaluation cycle.

### 10.4 Branch Prediction

When branch prediction is enabled, the stage must:

- use the predicted-taken information supplied with the fetched instruction;
- provide the not-taken fall-through address for misprediction recovery;
- avoid duplicate redirect requests for an already-handled predicted branch.

## 11. Performance and Status Indications

The stage must produce the following status/performance information:

- a jump execution event;
- a branch execution event;
- a taken-branch execution event;
- a stall event caused by waiting for the data memory side;
- a stall event caused by a multiply operation;
- a stall event caused by a divide operation;
- an instruction-completion event;
- a retired-instruction count qualifier.

The retired-instruction qualifier must be asserted when an instruction would normally be counted as retired. It must not be asserted for:

- environment-call instructions;
- breakpoint instructions;
- illegal instructions;
- instructions with illegal CSR accesses;
- instructions with fetch errors.

The stage must also provide a core-busy indication while the processor is not in its normal running state, such as during reset, exception handling, debug entry, or sleep.

## 12. Reset Behavior

After reset:

- the stage must be in its first-cycle execution state;
- no instruction may be considered executing;
- no memory request, CSR access, register write, branch redirect, or jump redirect may be issued;
- all multi-cycle operation storage must be cleared;
- completion and performance event indications must be deasserted.

The stage must resume normal operation only when a valid fetched instruction is presented and execution control allows it to proceed.
